## ABSTRACT
Multivariate metal-organic frameworks (MTV-MOFs) incorporating multiple chemical functionalities within single-phase crystalline materials, show superior properties that arise from synergistic effects. Herein, we report an efficient and versatile method for the growth of highly oriented multivariate surface-attached MOFs (MTV-SURMOFs) by the combination of the liquid-epitaxial growth method (LPE) and the mixed-linker strategy. Twenty-six MTVSURMOFs of the $[\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{2}\mathrm{P}]$ type with a maximum of five different dicarboxylate linkers (L) were deposited onto suitably functionalized surfaces. Systematic studies by IRRA spectroscopy and surface XRD provide evidence for the formation of highly oriented MTVSURMOFs. Interestingly, the $p K_{a}s$ of the dicarboxylate linkers play a crucial role for the orientational quality of the (MTV)-SURMOFs. In addition, benzene uptake experiments showed that the MTV-SURMOFs exhibit up to 2.6 times higher adsorption capacity as compared to the single-linker SURMOFs, demonstrating the synergistic effects in these surface systems.
Keywords: Multivariate metal-organic frameworks, liquid-phase epitaxial growth, heterogeneity, IRRAS, synergistic effect
## INTRODUCTION
The introduction of advanced complexity (e.g. multiple functional groups, heterogeneity) into single-phase crystalline materials remains challenging, because the complexity often leads to a mixture of phases (rather than a single phase) or to amorphous materials.1 The outstanding architecture of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) offers new chances in this field, as they are highly porous crystalline materials built from inorganic clusters and organic linkers. The hybrid nature and structural diversity of MOFs enable novel ways of control over pore metrics and functionalities. MOFs bearing multiple functional groups are of particular interest as these functional groups can serve as potential active sites for gas adsorption, drug release, photoluminescence, catalysis and molecular recognition.2-6 Remarkably, by sophisticated linker design (e.g., linkers sharing the same backbone but distinct functional groups) and judicious choice of the inorganic clusters, multiple functional groups can be installed into a single-phase MOF. This strategy was beautifully demonstrated by Yaghi and co-workers, who (randomly) incorporated up to eight different functional groups into a pure phase MOF-5, yielding a family of isoreticular multivariate MOFs (MTV-MOFs), sharing the same crystal topology as MOF-5.1 Interestingly, the MTV-MOFs exhibit superior adsorption properties outperforming the linear combinations of their corresponding single linker counterparts. Alternatively, the employment of two or three topologically distinct linkers decorated with multiple functional groups also has proven invaluable for building MTV-MOFs. For example, Telfer et al. demonstrated that by deliberate design, multi-component MOFs can be synthesized with utility of three linkers with different size, connectivity and symmetry.7 The disadvantage of this methodology is that MOFs meeting the criteria are rare. Later on, post-synthetic modification (PSM) and sequential linker installation were also explored for the preparation of MTV-MOFs.8,9 However, the above mentioned bulk MTV-MOFs are typically synthesized under one-pot solvothermal conditions, what often is not compatible with thermally sensitive functional groups.10-12 In addition, for certain linker mixtures, not all of the arbitrary combinations with regard to numbers and functionalities result in the corresponding MTVMOFs by solvothermal synthesis. In other words, the growth mechanism of MTV-MOFs, particularly concerning the physical and chemical properties (e.g., size, acidity, and redox potential) of the functional linkers, remains elusive.
In extension of the use of bulk MOF powders, the controlled deposition of MOF thin films permits their application in electronic devices, smart membranes, sensors, and photovoltaics.13-17 Among the various methods for the fabrication of MOFs thin films, the liquid-phase epitaxy (LPE), established by Wöll and co-workers, is of particular interest.18-20 The LPE method presents the following advantages as compared with commonly employed one-pot solvothermal methods: (i) the thickness, crystal orientation, and roughness of the obtained MOF films (denoted as SURMOFs in the following) is often highly controllable; (ii) the sequentially layer-by-layer deposition provides insights into the growth mechanism of MOFs; (iii) it occurs under mild conditions, typically in the range of room temperature to 60 $^\mathrm{{{\circ}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ using ethanol as solvent, and (iv), in some cases, allows the growth of elusive MOFs (e.g., non-interpenetrated MOFs, core-shell MOFs, core-shell-shell MOFs) that are not accessible or at least difficult to obtain under solvothermal conditions.21-25 We envisioned that LPE may be a general and efficient method for the growth of multivariate SURMOFs (MTV-SURMOFs), simply by using the mixed-linker strategy inspired by Yaghi’s work.1 However, unlike the characterization of a single crystalline MTV-MOF by high-resolution XRD and solid-state NMR spectroscopy,1,26 distinguishing a single-phase MTV-SURMOF (also called solidsolution or isomorphous structure,27 meaning that the linkers are mixed randomly within each crystal) from a physical mixture of SURMOFs with distinct chemical domains still remains a daunting challenge.
To this end, we have chosen a prototypical $[\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{2}\mathrm{P}]$ $\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ or $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ; $\mathrm{{L}=}$ dicarboxylate linker; ${\bf P}=$ dinitrogen pillar) MOF system as a model for the construction of MTV-SURMOFs by using the mixed-linker strategy.28-31 A variety of $[\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{2}\mathrm{P}]$ based SURMOFs have been successfully deposited on surfaces functionalized with self-assembled monolayers (SAMs) from readily available dicarboxylate linkers with diverse functionalities. The tetragonal network of $[\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{2}\mathrm{P}]$ MOFs allows precise control over the orientation of SURMOFs, which typically grow either along the [100] or [001] direction, depending on the surface properties of the SAMs. This can dramatically simplify the surface XRD (SXRD) analysis if the respective MTV-SURMOFs adopt the same growth orientation.20,23 More importantly, our previous study indicated that the orientational distribution in $[\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{2}\mathrm{P}]$ SURMOFs can be deduced from the symmetric/asymmetric stretch vibrations of -COO- of the dicarboxylate linkers by infrared reflection absorption spectroscopy (IRRAS), benefiting from the extremely high sensitivity of IRRAS.25,32 Recently, Centrone et al.33 demonstrated that a combination of infrared spectroscopy with atomic force microscopy (AFM-IR) can be used to determine the chemical heterogeneity of an individual MTV-MOF microcrystal. With these ideas in mind, we aim to grow $[\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{2}\mathrm{P}]$ MTV-SURMOFs on SAMs functionalized surfaces by the LPE method and wish to provide insights into the growth mechanism of MTV-SURMOFs using IRRAS and SXRD. As a proof of concept, herein we have chosen $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(b d c)_{2}d a b c o_{\frac{-}{\hbar}}}$ ] ( $\operatorname{bdc}{=}1,4{\mathrm{-}}$ 一 benzenedicarboxylate, dabco $=1{,}4$ -diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane) as a parent SURMOF, and demonstrated the successful growth of twenty-six MTV-SURMOFs with a maximum of five different dicarboxylate linkers ( $\mathrm{\tilde{C}_{4}H_{4}}$ -bdc, bdc, Br-bdc, $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ -bdc, and $\mathrm{F}_{4}$ -bdc, and hereafter denoted as A, B, C, D, and E，respectively, see Scheme 1.) installed into a single-phase MTVSURMOF. Interestingly, the crystal orientation of (MTV)-SURMOFs can be correlated to the (average) ${}p K_{a}$ value of the linker (mixtures), and nineteen highly [001] oriented MTVSURMOFs were obtained. The benzene uptake experiments indicated that some of the MTVSURMOFs and in particular the SURMOF-BCD show much higher adsorption capacity than the SURMOFs of the corresponding single-linker counterparts.
