## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
## $p K_{a}$ effects on the crystal orientation of individual SURMOFs
To examine the ability of $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}L_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ $\mathrm{{{L}=}}$ individual dicarboxylate linker of A, B, C, D, and E as shown in Scheme 1) to form isoreticular SURMOFs, we first conducted the growth of SURMOFs by using individual dicarboxylate linkers. The LPE process was performed by alternative immersion of a PPP1 ((4-(4-(4-pyridyl)phenyl)phenyl) methanethiol, molecule structure see Scheme 1) functionalized Au substrate into a $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{OAc})_{4}$ solution ( $\mathrm{1\mM\Cu^{2+}}$ in ethanol) and an organic linker solution (equimolar mixture of L and dabco, $0.1~\mathrm{mM}$ each in ethanol) at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , separated by ethanol washing steps. After growth for 40 cycles, SXRD patterns and IRRA spectra were recorded as displayed in Figure 1. Interestingly, the SXRD results indicated that the crystal orientation of SURMOFs was influenced mainly by the substituents on the bdc linker (that is, their electron-withdrawing properties). In the cases of SURMOF-A ( $\mathrm{\tilde{C}_{4}H_{4}}$ -bdc) and SURMOF-B (bdc), exclusively the (001) diffraction peaks were observed in the SXRD patterns, just as expected for the template effect of PPP1 (see Figure S1 for expected coordination mode). However, linkers with electron-withdrawing groups (-Br, - $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{-F_{4}}$ ) all produced partly [100] mis-oriented SURMOFs (marked with grey column in Figure 1a). Figure 1b displays the typical IRRA spectra of SURMOF-A to SURMOF-E in the regime of asymmetric $(1600-1650\mathrm{cm^{-1}})$ ) and symmetric -COO- stretching vibration $(\sim1400\mathrm{cm}^{-}$ 1). According to the IR selection rules on metallic surface (see Figure S1),34 the signals of the symmetric -COO- vibration in the IRRA spectra of SURMOF-A and SURMOF-B almost completely disappeared, suggesting that the $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(C O O)_{4}N_{2}]}$ units adopt the expected alignment (Cu-Cu axis perpendicular to metal surface, Figure S1). These results are in agreement with the SXRD results. The increased intensity of the symmetric -COO- peaks in the spectra of SURMOF-C, D, and E indicates an increased number of mis-aligned (Cu-Cu axis parallel to metal surface) $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(C O O)_{4}N_{2}]}$ units. In other words, the fractions of [100] oriented SURMOF crystals are increased. As the crystal orientation of $[\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{2}\mathrm{P}]$ MOFs is unambiguously associated with the orientation of the $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(C O O)_{4}N_{2}]}$ units, the fraction of [100] oriented crystals (mis-oriented crystals in this case) can be quantified from the SXRD and IRRAS, respectively. By plotting the fraction of [100] units versus $p K_{a}$ value of individual dicarboxylate linker, both methods offer similar results.35-37 Interestingly, the $p K_{a}$ value of the dicarboxylate linker readily correlates with the crystal orientation of SURMOFs. Linkers with high $p K_{a}\mathrm{s}$ tend to grow highly [001] oriented SURMOFs. When the $p K_{a}$ value of dicarboxylate linker is lower than a certain value, mis-oriented SURMOF crystals emerge. This conclusion was further supported by the SEM images (Figure 1d, e) of the SURMOFs. The highly [001] oriented SURMOF-A consists of densely packed square nanoplates with a uniform size of 150 nm. For SURMOF-B, dense films consisting of close-packed, intergrown nanocrystals were observed (Figure S43). For SURMOF-C and SURMOF-D, square nanocrystals were observed again; however, the number of mis-aligned nanocrystals, for example, tilted or lying tetragonal nanoprisms (marked with red arrows in Figure S43), increased with decreasing $p K_{a}$ of the dicarboxylate linker. In the case of SURMOF-E, larger tetragonal nanoprisms (about $450\mathrm{nm}$ ) with two types of orientation (mainly flat-lying accompanied with few upright ones) are visible. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first report that the crystal orientation of SURMOFs is correlated to the $p K_{a}$ value of the carboxylate linkers.
Note that the asymmetric -COO- peaks (dashed box in Figure 1b) of SURMOFs become blueshifted by up to $40\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ when going from SURMOF-A $(1621\mathrm{cm^{-1}})$ ) to SURMOF-E $(1661\mathrm{cm^{-1}})$ . The blue shift in IRRAS with respect to the substituent at the bdc linkers follow the same order as the $p K_{a}$ values, which can be attributed to the electron withdrawing effect of the substituents. We will show that the high sensitivity of IRRAS toward the substituent of bdc linkers is an efficient and accurate way to evaluate the chemical composition of MTV-SURMOFs, and consequently, provides evidences that MTV-SURMOFs fabricated by LPE method coupled with the mixed-linker strategy are indeed single-phase SURMOF “solid- solutions” rather than physical mixtures of SURMOFs.
## Characterization of binary MTV-SURMOFs by IRRAS
It has been reported that heteroepitaxial MOF-on-MOF structures can be grown by the LPE method and we envisaged that these MOF-on-MOF structures bearing anisotropic ligands, from chemistry point of view, are ideal models of a physical mixture of SURMOFs since the chemical composition is differed in $c$ -direction.38,39 To this end, we have deposited SURMOFD onto pre-grown SURMOF-A, and vice versa, to obtain SURMOF-D-on-A (short: D-on-A) and SURMOF-A-on-D (short: A-on-D). To grow SURMOF-AD, a mixture containing equimolar amounts of linker A and D, and dabco was used for the standard LPE method. Interestingly, while pure SURMOF-D itself grows less [001] oriented SURMOF, either by depositing SURMOF-D on top of SURMOF-A (highly [001] oriented), or by using a mixture of linkers A and D, highly [001] oriented D-on-A or SURMOF-AD can be obtained (Figure 2a). In contrast to the tilted nanorods observed in SURMOF-D, D-on-A exhibits dense films composed of vertically aligned nanorods (Figure S44). The seeding layer effect with respect to the crystal orientation of SURMOFs is in line with previous reports.22,40 To the best of our knowledge, the improved orientational growth of SURMOFs induced by a mixture of linkers is observed for the first time. Because these SURMOFs possess the same cell parameters, no position differences either in (001) or (100) diffraction can be observed in SXRD, meaning that the differences in the crystallographic “phase” caused by appending groups are undetectable by SXRD. In contrast to this, the IRRAS can perfectly discriminate between the different situations as shown in Figure 2b. The IRRA spectra of A-on-D and D-on-A both consist of two distinct peaks, which can be assigned to the asymmetric -COO- vibrations from SURMOFs containing only linker A $(1621~\mathrm{cm^{-1}})$ ) or linker D $(1644~\mathrm{cm^{-1}})$ ), respectively. In the MOF-onMOF structures, the functionalized bdc linkers are spatially separated. As a result, the interaction between the different carboxyl groups was negligible and individual asymmetric - COO- peaks originating from linker A and $\mathrm{~D~}$ were observed. This situation dramatically changed in the case of SURMOF-AD, where only one vibration peak located in between the positions for pure SURMOF-A and -D was observed. This IRRAS result is analogous to XRD patterns seen for metal alloys where only one diffraction peak located between the diffraction peaks from individual metals is observed. Therefore, we suggest that this single-peak feature of asymmetric -COO- vibration can be a criterion for the formation of a MTV-SURMOF solidsolution. The formation of single asymmetric -COO- peaks in MTV-SURMOFs is likely attributed to the random distribution (homogeneity) of A and D linkers in the crystal cell, giving rise to strong coupling between the vibration of the carboxylate groups due to the relative small cell parameters of $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}\mathrm{dabco}]$ $\scriptstyle({\mathrm{a=b=l}}0.92$ Å, $\scriptstyle\mathtt{c=9.61}$ Å).41,42 On closer inspection (Figure S10), the IRRA spectrum of SURMOF-AD also contains the characteristic peaks from individual linkers A (e.g., the C-C vibration of the naphthalene ring at $1516\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ) and D (e.g., the asymmetric vibration of - $\mathbf{\cdotNO}_{2}$ at $1543\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ), further validating the successful incorporation of A and D linkers into the SURMOF-AD. To further demonstrate the importance of mixing linkers at molecular scales, IRRA spectra of the deprotonated salts of linkers A and D as well as a physically well-ground mixture were recorded. Basically, the asymmetric -COO- peaks in the mixture represents a superposition of the signals from each linker as shown in Figure S4. Therefore, we conclude that from the view point of the homogeneity of chemical composition, only SURMOF-AD possesses a single-phase feature (Figure 2d). On the contrary, both A-onD and D-on-A are biphasic SURMOFs (Figure 2c, e). Note that the random distribution in SURMOF-AD only took place between linker A and D, and the dabco ligand was not involved because it was fixed in $c$ -direction, as confirmed by the high [001] orientation of SURMOFAD. From the discussion above, we learn that the high sensitivity of IRRAS enables us to elucidate the chemical composition of MTV-SURMOFs, thus offering convincing evidence for the formation of MTV-SURMOF solid-solutions.
A-on-D, and SURMOF-D-on-A. Schematic illustration of single-phase SURMOF-AD (d) and biphasic SURMOF-D-on-A (c) and SURMOF-A-on-D (e), with respect to the distribution of dicarboxylate linkers.
To learn more about the structure of MTV-SURMOFs, a series of SURMOF-AD with various A/D feeding ratios were prepared. SXRD results suggest that the crystal orientation of SURMOF- $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\cdot\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{D}_{1-\mathbf{x}}}$ depends on the feeding ratio of linker A and D. For SURMOF- $\Lambda_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{D}_{1-\mathrm{x}}(\mathrm{x}\ge0.5,$ A/D feeding ratio ${\geq}1$ ), exclusively [001] oriented SURMOFs were obtained. When the low $p K_{a}$ linker D dominated in the mixture, some [100] mis-oriented  SURMOF- $\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{D}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ $\mathrm{\acute{x}}^{<0.5}$ , A/D feeding ratio $^{<1}$ ) became deposited. We also found that the highest content of mis-oriented [100] units $(12\%)$ occurred in SURMOF- ${\bf\cdot A}_{0.2}\mathrm{D}_{0.8}$ , instead for the pure SURMOF-D. This result hinted on that besides $p K_{a}$ effect, crystal growth dynamics (e.g., ripening or flipping of SBUs) might be also involved in controlling the crystal orientation of SURMOFs as reported previously.25,32 SEM images revealed that SURMOF- $\mathrm{\cdotA}_{0.8}\mathrm{D}_{0.2}$ and SURMOF- ${\bf\cdot A}_{0.5}\mathrm{D}_{0.5}$ consisted of square-like nanoplates (Figure 3e,f), similar to SURMOF-A. In the case of SURMOF$\mathrm{A}_{0.2}\mathrm{D}_{0.8}$ , nanorods with somewhat tilted orientation (likely responsible for the mis-orientation seen in the spectra) were observed (Figure $3\mathrm{g}$ ). The varied ratio of dicarboxylate linkers in SURMOF- $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\cdot\mathbf{A}_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{D}_{1-\mathbf{x}}}$ was further confirmed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The high resolution $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ 1s spectra (Figure S34) recorded from SURMOF-D show two peaks located at $399.7\mathrm{eV}$ and $406.2\mathrm{eV}$ , which are characteristic N 1s signals for dabco and $\left.-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right.$ , respectively. In SURMOF- $\mathbf{\partial}\cdot\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathbf{D}_{1-\mathrm{x}},$ , the intensity of $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ 1s originated from $\left.-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right.$ gradually decreased with increasing the portion of linker A from 0.2 to 0.8. The actual ratio of D/A calculated from IRRAS and XPS shows very similar tendency, validating each other.
Independent of the feeding ratios, all the SURMOF- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{A_{x}D_{1-x}}$ display only one asymmetric -COOvibration peak located in between the ones of SURMOF-A and SURMOF-D, implying the single-phase nature of SURMOF- $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathrm{A_{x}D_{1-x}}}$ . Moreover, the data suggest that the chemical composition of binary SURMOFs can be tuned by the feeding ratio of two dicarboxylate linkers as the obtained SURMOFs are truly “solid-solutions”, as there is an approximately linear relationship between the changes of wavenumber and the mole fraction of linker D (Figure 2d, black squares). In other words, for binary SURMOFs, the analysis of the shift in asymmetric - COO- vibration enables us to estimate the actual ratio of individual dicarboxylate linker incorporated in SURMOFs. We will show later this unprecedented IR shifting effect is useful to calculate the actual linker ratio in arbitrary binary SURMOFs, particularly when the two dicarboxylate linkers show big differences in coordination ability to the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ SBU.
## Growth behavior of binary SURMOFs
We then turned our attention to grow arbitrary binary SURMOFs by mixing equal molar amounts of a variety of dicarboxylate linkers under identical conditions. Ten different MTVSURMOFs were grown from the binary mixtures of five individual dicarboxylate linkers. SXRD and IRRAS results clearly demonstrated that the crystal orientation of MTV-SURMOFs is correlated to the applied combination of linkers. Briefly, the mixtures of dicarboxylate linkers with high $p K_{a}$ values typically result in highly [001] oriented binary SURMOFs such as SURMOF-AB, AC, AD, and BC. In contrast, binary SURMOFs with a significant amount of [100] oriented crystals are obtained from the combination of dicarboxylate linkers with low ${}p K_{a}$ values. Generally, the intensity of either (100) diffraction peaks or symmetric -COOvibration peaks seems to increase with decreasing $p K_{a}$ value of the mixed binary systems. As described above, the single-phase nature of binary MTV-SURMOFs is evidenced by the presence of only a single asymmetric -COO- vibration peak in each case (Figure 4b and Figure S7-16, left spectra). It is nevertheless worthy to note that for the SURMOF-AE, a small shoulder in the asymmetric -COO- peak is visible, and this peak can be deconvoluted into two peaks centered at $1656~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and $1621~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ . Careful comparison of these two peaks with those of SURMOF-A, SURMOF-E, and SURMOF-E-on-A, led to the conclusion that some pure SURMOF-A might be present in the SURMOF-AE system.
As mentioned above, the IRRA spectra do not only provide orientational information, but also show characteristic peaks of the individual dicarboxylate linkers, e.g., $\mathsf{\Delta}\mathsf{\Psi}\mathsf{v}_{\mathsf{C-C}}$ of the naphthalene ring in linker A, at $1516~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ; $\mathsf{u}_{\mathrm{C-Br}}$ in linker C, at $1041~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ; $\mathsf{u}_{-\mathrm{NO}_{2}}$ in linker D, at $1543~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ; υC-F in linker E, at $995~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , confirming the successful incorporation of both kinds of linkers (details see Figure S7-16, right spectra, marked with arrows in each spectrum). Despite the same feeding ratio (1:1) of mixed linkers was used for the growth of all ten binary SURMOFs, the shift of the asymmetric -COO- vibration peak is not with the simple average of the shift of the parent SURMOFs, hinting on a deviation from the 1:1 ratio in the MTVSURMOFs. As stated before, we are able to estimate the actual ratio of the individual linkers in binary MTV-SURMOFs by the shift of the asymmetric -COO- peak, and the results are summarized in Figure S7-16. Typically, the amount of the lower $p K_{a}$ linker was found to be in the range of $53-75\%$ , thus constituting the major component. Notably, in a few cases, for example, SURMOF-BD and SURMOF-BE, the amount of linker D and E is disproportionately higher (up to $90\%$ ) than that of linker B, suggesting a disfavored interaction between B and D or E, respectively. This effect has also been observed in the synthesis of bulk MTV-MOFs, and for some combinations of linkers, solvothermal synthesis could not produce the corresponding bulk MTV-MOFs at all.1 A reasonable explanation can be the competitive coordination between dicarboxylate linkers at the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ SBU.43 Because the low $p K_{a}$ linker is more easily deprotonated than a high $p K_{a}$ one, the low $p K_{a}$ linker can be more readily bound to the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ SBU, resulting in higher portion of low $p K_{a}$ linker in binary SURMOFs. However, the binding of low $p K_{a}$ linkers at $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ SBUs also brings disadvantages regarding the crystal orientation within the SURMOFs. For example, a high deposition rate might lead to the formation of defects (e.g., flipping of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ SBUs) during crystal growth, and result in mis-oriented SURMOFs in the end. SEM images in Figure 4 provide morphological evidences of defect formation in SURMOF-DE. Compared to SURMOF-AB, where densely packed square nanoplates were found, SURMOF-DE displayed larger crystals and a few mis-oriented crystals (marked by red arrows). In order to quantitatively analyze the $p K_{a}$ effect for crystal orientation in binary SURMOFs systems, we introduced an average ${}p K_{a}$ mix value of the binary dicarboxylate linker systems based on the actual portion of the dicarboxylate linkers in the MTV-SURMOFs as derived from the IRRAS data (details see SI). Note that this ${p/{K_{a}}^{m i x}}$ has no physical meaning, but can be used to predict the crystal orientation in the MTV-SURMOFs. By plotting the fraction of mis-oriented [100] units versus ${p K_{a}}^{m i x}$ value of the binary system (Figure 4c), a linear relationship between [100] units and calculated ${p}K_{a}{}^{m i x}$ was observed, similarly as in the single dicarboxylate SURMOFs (Figure 1c). Perfectly [001] oriented binary SURMOFs can be achieved only, if the ${p K_{a}}^{m i x}$ is higher than a specific value (presumably the intersection point of the blue and red dashed lines in Figure 4c, which is at 2.5 in this case).
## Growth of ternary, quaternary, and quinary SURMOFs
To demonstrate the generality of the LPE method in preparation of MTV-SURMOFs, we conducted the growth of MTV-SURMOFs on PPP1 functionalized surface by using ternary, quaternary, and quinary mixtures of dicarboxylate linkers, respectively. As shown in Figure 5, except for the ternary SURMOF-CDE, the MTV-SURMOFs are highly [001] oriented. It seems that the more dicarboxylate linkers were present in the deposition solution, the better [001] oriented MTV-SURMOFs were grown on the substrates. Additionally, the morphology of the MTV-SURMOFs became more similar (densely packed nanoplates, Figures 5 d,h,l). The previously discussed blue shift of the asymmetric -COO- vibration peak was also observed in each set of MTV-SUMOFs, for example, in SURMOF-ABC, SURMOF-ABD, and SURMOFABE, following the same order with increasing ${p}K_{a}{}^{m i x}$ . As before, in all these MTV-SURMOFs, we only observed a single and symmetric peak for the asymmetric -COO- vibration (dashed curves in Figure ${5\mathrm{~c,g,k}})$ , which differed from their single component SURMOFs, indicating the formation of MTV-SURMOFs. Other characteristic peaks from the individual dicarboxylate linker can also be identified in MTV-SURMOFs, providing further evidence of the successful incorporation of multiple linkers in the MTV-SURMOFs (Figure S17-32). In ternary, quaternary, and quinary SURMOFs systems, we were unable to calculate the ratio of individual dicarboxylate linker from IRRA spectra based on the shift of asymmetric -COOvibration peaks. The successful incorporation of multiple linkers in MTV-SURMOFs was further confirmed by XP spectra (Figure S35-42), which revealed the presence of the respective Br 3d, N 1s, and F 1s signals in the ternary, quaternary, and quinary SURMOFs.
