## Adsorption properties of MTV-SURMOFs
The highly oriented MTV-SURMOFs with their variety of functional groups offer unique adsorption properties toward guest molecules and motivated us to examine the adsorption capacity of MTV-SURMOFs. To do this, the five single-linker SURMOFs and ten MTVSURMOFs were grown onto a PPP1-modified Au electrode of a quartz crystal microbalance (QCM) for 40 cycles. Benzene was selected as a probe molecule since its van der Waals diameter $(5.35\mathrm{~\AA})$ matches well to the pore opening of the $[\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{2}\mathrm{P}]$ SURMOFs.44 As shown in Figure 6a, the benzene dynamic uptake curves indicate that the functional groups in SURMOFs play significant roles for both the adsorption capacity and the rate. Despite SURMOF-A and SURMOF-B both being highly [001] oriented, SURMOF-B shows a dramatic mass uptake within 5 seconds and a maximum adsorption capacity of $58\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was achieved. In contrast, it takes at least 5 minutes for SURMOF-A to reach a plateau with a low adsorption capacity of only $16\mathrm{mgg^{-1}}$ . Because the unit cells of SURMOF-A and SURMOF-B are identical, the addition of a phenyl ring significantly decreases the accessible pore volume of SURMOFA. Moreover, the naphthalene ring unit also acts as gate (as it is able to rotate in the pore) for the entrance of guest molecules, causing mass transfer resistance. The adsorption capacity and dynamics of the other three SURMOFs are somewhere in between of SURMOF-A and SURMOF-B. As reported previously,32 the orientation of the SURMOFs does not play a significant role for adsorption capacity and dynamic rate of individual SURMOFs, as these values are rather dominated by the accessible pore volume and hindrance of appended functional groups than by the direction of pore opening, as can be seen for the most disordered SURMOF-E. Interestingly, for the ten investigated MTV-SURMOFs, except for SURMOFAB and SURMOF-BE, which show lower benzene adsorption capacitance than SURMOF-B, all other SURMOFs display a significantly higher benzene adsorption capacity (Figure S49- 52). For example, the maximum adsorption capacity of $97~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ for SURMOF-BCD is reached within ten minutes, which is 1.7 times higher than for SURMOF-B, and 2.6 times higher than for the average of SURMOF-A to E $\left(38~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}\right)$ , calculated from Figure 6a). We also note that MTV-SURMOFs containing linker A typically show a low dynamic adsorption rate, what can be attributed to the steric hindrance induced by the naphthalene ring, causing slow intracrystalline diffusion of the guest molecules.45 A great advantage of MTV-MOFs is that the synergistic effect emanating from the multiple functional groups could significantly improve the physical and chemical properties.46 In our case, we believe that the appropriate combination of dicarboxylate linkers provides a just right average pore size to accommodate the benzene molecules, therefore, achieving enhanced adsorption capacity. Our LPE method thus opens a new route for the fine tuning of the chemical and physical properties of SURMOFs.
(b). The uptake differs on both, the kinetics as well as the maximum amount. Observe that the scales are the same in both diagrams, illustrating the significantly improved properties of the MTV-SURMOFs.
## CONCLUSION
In conclusion, we have demonstrated that LPE is an efficient and versatile method for the growth of highly oriented MTV-SURMOF solid-solutions by simply using a mixed-linker strategy. IRRA spectroscopy proved to be the most informative technique in measuring the chemical composition (e.g., ratio of mixed binary linkers) and crystal orientation of (MTV)- SURMOFs based on the comprehensive studies of the symmetric and asymmetric -COOvibration peaks from dicarboxylate linkers. More importantly, IRRAS allows the identification of a single-phase nature of MTV-SURMOF built from a series of dicarboxylate linkers with diverse functional groups. The (average) ${}p K_{a}$ value of either individual dicarboxylate linker or mixed-linker systems was found to be a key factor for the determination of the crystal orientation of the (MTV)-SURMOFs. Highly [001] oriented (MTV)-SURMOFs can only be attained, if the $p K_{a}$ value is higher than a certain value, in the current case about 2.5. The formation of MTV-SURMOFs is a result of competitive coordination of the dicarboxylate linkers to the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ SBUs. Interestingly, with more dicarboxylate linkers in solution, the orientational quality of the MTV-SURMOFs improved, suggesting an entropy-enthalpy compensation effect. Benzene uptake experiments demonstrated that most of the MTVSURMOFs exhibit much higher adsorption capacity as compared the corresponding singlelinker counterparts. This opens the possibility for design of high-capacity storage media by carefully adjusting the mix of the ligands. In addition, the mild conditions of the LPE method will enable the preparation of MTV-MOFs bearing fragile and/or reactive functional groups (e.g., nitroxide radicals), which would otherwise be destroyed under typical solvothermal conditions.
